  the tracker. [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m exports [38;5;153mtrackLogin(userId)[39m. [38;5;153msrc/config.js[39m holds both URLs — the single swap point when
  the real endpoints exist. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes thin DOM glue loaded as [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m. Tests import [38;5;153mauth.js[39m directly and pass
  a fake [38;5;153mfetch[39m.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m real module boundaries, everything verifiable, the swap point is one file. Costs an [38;5;153mindex.html[39m change and
  forces a decision on module format, since [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS today and this wants ESM. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mB. Single file, guarded export seam[0m
  Everything stays in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Add a [38;5;153mtypeof module !== "undefined"[39m export at the bottom and wrap the DOM wiring so it
  doesn't run under Node. Tests [38;5;153mrequire("./app.js")[39m and inject a fake [38;5;153mfetch[39m.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m smallest diff, no [38;5;153mindex.html[39m change, no module-format decision. But the guards exist purely to make the
  file testable — that's scaffolding, not design — and auth, analytics, validation, and DOM wiring stay in one file
  that's about to grow. [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mC. Decouple analytics from auth[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m stays single-purpose: authenticate, return [38;5;153m{ success, userId }[39m. It never knows analytics exists. The submit
  handler — or a small emitter — routes the result to [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the cleanest separation, and adding a second tracked event later costs nothing. But every future caller of
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m has to remember to track, so the thing you actually asked for ("track who logged in") becomes opt-in and
  silently skippable. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's the only one where the tracking can't be forgotten, the fakes have a real seam to plug into, and
  the "swap in real URLs later" promise lands in one file. B is cheaper today but spends its savings on test scaffolding
  you'd delete when the file splits — which this feature is already pushing it toward. C's decoupling is genuinely
  better design in a larger app; here it trades a guarantee for flexibility you have no second use for yet.
